So thank you for joining.

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:37.430
RAIA HADSELL: Thanks, Melanie.

00:03:37.430 --> 00:03:39.513
MELANIE WARRICK: So Raia,
can you tell us a little

00:03:39.513 --> 00:03:42.839
about yourself, your
background in this space?

00:03:42.839 --> 00:03:43.630
RAIA HADSELL: Sure.

00:03:43.630 --> 00:03:46.670
I've been at DeepMind
for the last four years.

00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:49.490
I lead one of our research
groups in the deep learning

00:03:49.490 --> 00:03:53.180
area, work on problems in
robotics and reinforcement

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:55.850
learning, lifelong learning,
a bunch of different subjects

00:03:55.850 --> 00:03:57.530
that I'm excited about.

00:03:57.530 --> 00:04:00.527
Before this I worked
at SRI International.

00:04:00.527 --> 00:04:02.360
I was at Carnegie Mellon
for a little while,

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:04.730
and before that I was at
New York University, where

00:04:04.730 --> 00:04:06.260
I did my PhD with Yann LeCun.

00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:08.810
So a lot of neural
networks trained

00:04:08.810 --> 00:04:11.204
in different ways over the
years for different things.

00:04:11.204 --> 00:04:12.620
MELANIE WARRICK:
What inspired you

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:15.080
to go into robotics
research to begin with?

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:17.240
RAIA HADSELL: I
really loved the idea

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:20.430
of writing code and
coming up with algorithms,

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:22.280
but then going out
in the real world

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:24.440
and seeing if they
worked or not.

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:27.950
If I did robotics, then I
could take my algorithms out,

00:04:27.950 --> 00:04:31.010
I could run them on a
mobile robot that we had,

00:04:31.010 --> 00:04:33.710
and I could see whether or not
the robot ran into the tree

00:04:33.710 --> 00:04:35.450
or went around the tree.

00:04:35.450 --> 00:04:39.440
First problem of mobile
robotics, avoid the trees.

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:42.710
Robotics brought me into machine
learning because we really

00:04:42.710 --> 00:04:46.170
wanted ways of making the
robot behavior be adaptive.

00:04:46.170 --> 00:04:48.890
I didn't want to run
the robots on paths

00:04:48.890 --> 00:04:51.850
that all looked the same
or on roads with markings.

00:04:51.850 --> 00:04:54.870
I wanted to run the
robots in the forest

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:57.740
or on paths in the grass
or through shrubbery, where

00:04:57.740 --> 00:05:00.379
it would have to identify
where the obstacles were

00:05:00.379 --> 00:05:01.670
and where the clear paths were.

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:03.128
And that required
machine learning.

00:05:03.128 --> 00:05:04.830
That required
adapting over time.

00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:08.115
MARK MANDEL: So that
sounds really interesting.

00:05:08.115 --> 00:05:09.990
I don't know anything
about machine learning.

00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:13.490
What allows you to do that
kind of thing that lets robots

00:05:13.490 --> 00:05:14.990
basically, what, see space?

00:05:14.990 --> 00:05:16.365
RAIA HADSELL: The
way that we did

00:05:16.365 --> 00:05:18.950
it then was to sort of
collect data all the time.

00:05:18.950 --> 00:05:21.320
We'd label that data
as to whether or not

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:23.510
it was the ground
or an obstacle,

00:05:23.510 --> 00:05:26.710
and then the robot would
learn that immediately.

00:05:26.710 --> 00:05:28.640
And it would train
a neural network

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:32.720
to make a decision, either
good to drive or an obstacle,

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:34.130
bad to drive there.

00:05:34.130 --> 00:05:36.980
And that worked well,
and it allowed the robot

00:05:36.980 --> 00:05:41.730
to adapt and to
change its decision.

00:05:41.730 --> 00:05:44.379
But what we actually do now
is reinforcement learning.

00:05:44.379 --> 00:05:45.920
Maybe I should
explain the difference

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:48.060
between reinforcement
learning and machine learning.

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:49.130
MARK MANDEL: Sounds great.

00:05:49.130 --> 00:05:50.880
RAIA HADSELL: People
are probably familiar

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:53.420
now with things that
we call AI systems that

00:05:53.420 --> 00:05:57.200
might do things like
recognize objects and images

00:05:57.200 --> 00:05:58.774
or help make some decisions.

00:05:58.774 --> 00:06:01.190
And that's done, usually, using
supervised learning, where

00:06:01.190 --> 00:06:04.670
we have a big data set
of, for, instance, images,

00:06:04.670 --> 00:06:06.966
and each of those images
has a label attached to it,

00:06:06.966 --> 00:06:08.340
like this is an
image of an apple

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:11.270
and this is an image
of a monkey, et cetera.

00:06:11.270 --> 00:06:15.980
And you train a neural network
to produce those labels

00:06:15.980 --> 00:06:18.862
by teaching it, and the data
set stays exactly the same.

00:06:18.862 --> 00:06:21.320
And that's really important to
make the learning work well.

00:06:21.320 --> 00:06:23.750
What if instead we
want our algorithm

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:26.580
to take actions in the world?

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:28.760
And the only feedback
we're going to give

00:06:28.760 --> 00:06:32.360
is a reward, either a positive
reward or a negative reward.

00:06:32.360 --> 00:06:35.390
So instead of giving labels
and sort of drawing data

00:06:35.390 --> 00:06:37.460
from a data set, we
want an algorithm

00:06:37.460 --> 00:06:39.070
that can interact
with the world,

00:06:39.070 --> 00:06:41.570
have feedback that comes as
positive or negative rewards,

00:06:41.570 --> 00:06:43.700
and change its
behavior based on that.

00:06:43.700 --> 00:06:46.100
That type of learning
is both more powerful,

00:06:46.100 --> 00:06:48.480
because then the algorithm's
actually interacting

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:50.490
with the world and
able to take actions,

00:06:50.490 --> 00:06:52.280
for instance,
driving instructions.

00:06:52.280 --> 00:06:53.810
But it's also a lot harder.

00:06:53.810 --> 00:06:56.289
Say I want my robot to be
able to drive around a room.

00:06:56.289 --> 00:06:57.830
It has to make all
of these decisions

00:06:57.830 --> 00:07:00.420
about when to turn left and when
to stop and when to go faster

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:01.378
and when to get slower.

00:07:01.378 --> 00:07:04.340
And then at the end I say,
that was good or that was bad.

00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:06.650
You hit things or you did well.

00:07:06.650 --> 00:07:10.960
And the algorithm then
needs to figure out, ah,

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:12.710
where did I made the
mistake, or where did

00:07:12.710 --> 00:07:13.918
I do the thing that was good?

00:07:13.918 --> 00:07:16.370
And it needs to do something
we call credit assignment.

00:07:16.370 --> 00:07:19.100
Get a good reward, and
I need to figure out

00:07:19.100 --> 00:07:22.190
how to change my neural
network to incorporate

00:07:22.190 --> 00:07:22.902
that information.

00:07:22.902 --> 00:07:24.860
And that's what we call
reinforcement learning,

00:07:24.860 --> 00:07:28.250
and it's very much the same
as the way in which we think

00:07:28.250 --> 00:07:30.440
that, for instance,
a dog learns to do

00:07:30.440 --> 00:07:33.960
a trick by using a positive
reward when it does the trick.

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:36.230
But it is quite different
from supervised learning

00:07:36.230 --> 00:07:37.820
or other types of
machine learning.

00:07:37.820 --> 00:07:39.560
MELANIE WARRICK: And
I know that DeepMind

00:07:39.560 --> 00:07:42.650
is well-known for the
experimentation and exploration

00:07:42.650 --> 00:07:43.600
with Atari games.

00:07:43.600 --> 00:07:45.020
This is sort of
a starting point,

00:07:45.020 --> 00:07:46.910
or a well-known
point when we start

00:07:46.910 --> 00:07:49.460
talking about reinforcement
learning, which games granted,

00:07:49.460 --> 00:07:52.310
Mark, is all your
area of expertise,

00:07:52.310 --> 00:07:54.110
so I know you love
that kind of stuff.

00:07:54.110 --> 00:07:57.230
One area you've been studying
is dealing with navigation.

00:07:57.230 --> 00:08:00.110
What helped drive the current
research that you have?

00:08:00.110 --> 00:08:03.350
I mean, it sounds like obviously
your interest in the space,

00:08:03.350 --> 00:08:06.574
your exploration around
spatial awareness in the past.

00:08:06.574 --> 00:08:08.990
How did you decide to go down
this path with reinforcement

00:08:08.990 --> 00:08:09.650
learning?

00:08:09.650 --> 00:08:11.750
RAIA HADSELL: For
things like Atari games,

00:08:11.750 --> 00:08:14.000
you don't need a lot of memory.

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:15.630
You need to learn
how to play the game.

00:08:15.630 --> 00:08:17.870
So we talk about an agent
learning to play the game.

00:08:17.870 --> 00:08:20.328
That's the usual term that we
use in reinforcement learning

00:08:20.328 --> 00:08:23.330
is that the algorithm
that's changing over time,

00:08:23.330 --> 00:08:25.850
is getting rewards,
is called the agent.

00:08:25.850 --> 00:08:28.340
And it interacts with
an environment, which

00:08:28.340 --> 00:08:30.650
could be an Atari game
or it could be a maze,

00:08:30.650 --> 00:08:32.360
in the case of navigation.

00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:36.080
And navigation really brings a
whole other level of difficulty

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:39.409
to it because you need
to have a memory of where

00:08:39.409 --> 00:08:41.210
have I been before?

00:08:41.210 --> 00:08:42.500
Where am I right now?

00:08:42.500 --> 00:08:44.420
Where am I trying to get to?

00:08:44.420 --> 00:08:46.874
So you need to incorporate
all of these different cues.

00:08:46.874 --> 00:08:48.290
And when I'm talking
about a maze,

00:08:48.290 --> 00:08:49.460
I'm not talking
about a maze that you

00:08:49.460 --> 00:08:51.020
get to see from the
outside, that you

00:08:51.020 --> 00:08:51.900
get to see the whole thing.

00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:52.399
No.

00:08:52.399 --> 00:08:54.590
Imagine a 3D hedge
maze where you can't

00:08:54.590 --> 00:08:56.420
see beyond your current space.

00:08:56.420 --> 00:09:00.650
And you need to try to have
that spatial representation,

00:09:00.650 --> 00:09:02.450
that spatial
understanding that says,

00:09:02.450 --> 00:09:03.890
I know where I've been
before and I know where

00:09:03.890 --> 00:09:05.014
I'm going to try to get to.

00:09:05.014 --> 00:09:07.620
And I'm going to try to learn
the path to get to that point.

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:10.700
So I just thought that it
was a really interesting area

00:09:10.700 --> 00:09:11.330
of research.

00:09:11.330 --> 00:09:12.720
It was very challenging.

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:14.510
And so we did some
research that showed

00:09:14.510 --> 00:09:18.650
that we could train our
algorithms to solve these 3D

00:09:18.650 --> 00:09:21.590
mazes, and that we could
train agents to do that where

00:09:21.590 --> 00:09:23.910
the mazes were sort of a game.

00:09:23.910 --> 00:09:26.930
We actually used the
Doom engine to create

00:09:26.930 --> 00:09:29.060
all of these different
mazes and try

00:09:29.060 --> 00:09:31.850
to just have the agent
get from wherever

00:09:31.850 --> 00:09:34.491
it spawned to a goal location.

00:09:34.491 --> 00:09:35.990
And if it gets to
the goal location,

00:09:35.990 --> 00:09:37.670
then it gets teleported
somewhere else

00:09:37.670 --> 00:09:39.336
and it needs to find
its way back again.

00:09:39.336 --> 00:09:41.540
So it both has to do
exploration as well as

00:09:41.540 --> 00:09:44.870
remembering where was the goal
and finding its way back there

00:09:44.870 --> 00:09:46.250
very quickly.

00:09:46.250 --> 00:09:48.440
And we trained algorithms,
we trained agents

00:09:48.440 --> 00:09:51.230
that were better at
doing this than a human,

00:09:51.230 --> 00:09:54.590
and we did that by incorporating
memory into the algorithms,

00:09:54.590 --> 00:09:56.030
into the neural network itself.

00:09:56.030 --> 00:09:57.904
And that's a bit different
than what we think

00:09:57.904 --> 00:09:59.120
about traditional robotics.

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:01.850
Algorithms for doing
navigation usually

00:10:01.850 --> 00:10:04.100
start by let's build
a map, and then we

00:10:04.100 --> 00:10:06.576
can run a path-planning
algorithm on the map.

00:10:06.576 --> 00:10:08.450
And then we can send
the control instructions

00:10:08.450 --> 00:10:11.730
to the robot to follow
that path through the map.

00:10:11.730 --> 00:10:15.140
Instead, here we're doing it
all intrinsically with instead

00:10:15.140 --> 00:10:18.900
of an explicit map, we have
an implicit representation.

00:10:18.900 --> 00:10:20.820
And that's much more the
way that we navigate,

00:10:20.820 --> 00:10:22.230
which I find really interesting.

00:10:22.230 --> 00:10:24.620
MARK MANDEL: So you talked
about both the simulation

00:10:24.620 --> 00:10:26.760
and robotics in the real world.

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:29.295
I'm guessing that you do your
training in the simulation?

00:10:29.295 --> 00:10:30.920
How does that work
with the translation

00:10:30.920 --> 00:10:31.820
between both things?

00:10:31.820 --> 00:10:33.620
I'm guessing the real world
is a little more complicated

00:10:33.620 --> 00:10:34.520
than the simulation?

00:10:34.520 --> 00:10:35.899
Are there challenges there?

00:10:35.899 --> 00:10:37.190
RAIA HADSELL: Yeah, absolutely.

00:10:37.190 --> 00:10:38.930
The real world is very hard.

00:10:38.930 --> 00:10:41.210
Robotics in general, with
reinforcement learning,

00:10:41.210 --> 00:10:42.350
is very hard.

00:10:42.350 --> 00:10:43.760
I mean, why is it hard?

00:10:43.760 --> 00:10:46.760
The world has a lot more
noise and complexity

00:10:46.760 --> 00:10:50.060
to it than the simulations
that we create.

00:10:50.060 --> 00:10:51.530
And that makes
these algorithms--

00:10:51.530 --> 00:10:54.560
which are visually based, right,
you're looking at an image--

00:10:54.560 --> 00:10:56.720
that makes it awfully
hard to operate.

00:10:56.720 --> 00:10:59.030
It's also hard because the
amount of data being used.

00:10:59.030 --> 00:11:03.680
So the agent that can learn
to navigate in mazes better

00:11:03.680 --> 00:11:07.880
than a human can learns
for over a billion steps

00:11:07.880 --> 00:11:10.250
in the network, which
means it's about a week

00:11:10.250 --> 00:11:12.049
of 24-hour training.

00:11:12.049 --> 00:11:14.090
And that's not just one
agent, but that's usually

00:11:14.090 --> 00:11:17.390
about 128 different
distributed agents

00:11:17.390 --> 00:11:18.474
learning at the same time.

00:11:18.474 --> 00:11:20.223
MELANIE WARRICK:
Multiple different agents

00:11:20.223 --> 00:11:22.760
distributed across the network
exploring similar spaces.

00:11:22.760 --> 00:11:24.551
RAIA HADSELL: Exactly
why we use simulation

00:11:24.551 --> 00:11:27.000
is because we can run again
and again, we can speed it up,

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:30.140
we can run it 24 hours a
day, and we can understand

00:11:30.140 --> 00:11:31.184
which algorithms work.

00:11:31.184 --> 00:11:33.350
MELANIE WARRICK: Just
curious, are using TensorFlow?

00:11:33.350 --> 00:11:34.055
RAIA HADSELL: Yes.

00:11:34.055 --> 00:11:34.971
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:11:34.971 --> 00:11:36.950
So in terms of the
work that you're doing,

00:11:36.950 --> 00:11:38.990
the streetwise navigation
research that you guys

00:11:38.990 --> 00:11:42.400
were doing, you were using some
real world data, is that right?

00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:44.150
RAIA HADSELL: So after
working for a while

00:11:44.150 --> 00:11:48.550
with these simulated mazes,
like I said, using Quake or Doom

00:11:48.550 --> 00:11:50.480
engine, they were really
good to work with,

00:11:50.480 --> 00:11:53.330
but I really felt like we
weren't really demonstrating

00:11:53.330 --> 00:11:55.952
that these algorithms would
work in the real world.

00:11:55.952 --> 00:11:57.410
And we wanted to
have at least some

00:11:57.410 --> 00:12:00.770
of that real world
noisiness and complexity.

00:12:00.770 --> 00:12:03.470
And somebody here that I
work with, Karen Simonyan,

00:12:03.470 --> 00:12:05.600
said we have Street View.

00:12:05.600 --> 00:12:09.530
And Street View is a really
interesting set of data.

00:12:09.530 --> 00:12:11.570
And probably everybody
who knows Street View

00:12:11.570 --> 00:12:13.160
has used it at some point.

00:12:13.160 --> 00:12:15.530
This gives you an
on the ground, sort

00:12:15.530 --> 00:12:20.060
of panoramic, interactive
interface with a map,

00:12:20.060 --> 00:12:21.170
with a city.

00:12:21.170 --> 00:12:25.250
And so we said what if we turned
this into an interactive RL

00:12:25.250 --> 00:12:28.220
environment and trained
our agents there?

00:12:28.220 --> 00:12:30.830
We took Street View in
an area-- for instance,

00:12:30.830 --> 00:12:33.200
all of New York City,
most of Manhattan--

00:12:33.200 --> 00:12:36.450
and we turned that into an
interactive environment.

00:12:36.450 --> 00:12:39.650
So on each frame, on every
step, then, the agent

00:12:39.650 --> 00:12:41.840
gets an image
that's just like you

00:12:41.840 --> 00:12:44.210
would see if you were using
Street View but without any

00:12:44.210 --> 00:12:46.220
of the arrows or
other information.

00:12:46.220 --> 00:12:48.230
And it gets to decide
on an action to take.

00:12:48.230 --> 00:12:50.480
It can turn in a circle,
it can look up and down,

00:12:50.480 --> 00:12:51.720
or it can take a step.

00:12:51.720 --> 00:12:54.260
And if it takes a step,
then it will move forward

00:12:54.260 --> 00:12:58.910
to the next panorama in Street
View if there is an edge there.

00:12:58.910 --> 00:13:01.520
And that allows the
agent to actually walk

00:13:01.520 --> 00:13:04.770
across New York City and
try to find different goals.

00:13:04.770 --> 00:13:06.920
So we made different
sorts of games here

00:13:06.920 --> 00:13:08.990
and tried using
the same algorithms

00:13:08.990 --> 00:13:11.672
to see if the agent could
learn to explore New York City.

00:13:11.672 --> 00:13:13.880
MELANIE WARRICK: And what
were some of the algorithms

00:13:13.880 --> 00:13:14.940
that you used?

00:13:14.940 --> 00:13:16.820
RAIA HADSELL: So we've
been mainly using

00:13:16.820 --> 00:13:18.980
an algorithm called
Impala, which

00:13:18.980 --> 00:13:22.670
is an actor critic method
of reinforcement learning.

00:13:22.670 --> 00:13:24.900
It works very well distributed.

00:13:24.900 --> 00:13:27.270
So you can think
about it as there

00:13:27.270 --> 00:13:31.157
are a bunch of different actors,
say 128 different agents.

00:13:31.157 --> 00:13:32.990
They've all got a copy
of the neural network

00:13:32.990 --> 00:13:34.906
that we're training, and
they're all somewhere

00:13:34.906 --> 00:13:37.820
different in New York City in
this virtual environment Street

00:13:37.820 --> 00:13:38.640
View.

00:13:38.640 --> 00:13:40.640
They're all in different
parts of New York City,

00:13:40.640 --> 00:13:43.446
and they're all trying to find
their way to different goals.

00:13:43.446 --> 00:13:44.820
And that's the
sort of game play.

00:13:44.820 --> 00:13:46.520
And then there's
one learner, and all

00:13:46.520 --> 00:13:49.460
of the updates to the neural
network come to that learner,

00:13:49.460 --> 00:13:51.560
and it puts them all
together in a nice way

00:13:51.560 --> 00:13:52.920
and learns over time.

00:13:52.920 --> 00:13:53.962
And that's the algorithm.

00:13:53.962 --> 00:13:56.211
MELANIE WARRICK: It's almost
making me think of, like,

00:13:56.211 --> 00:13:56.960
a scavenger hunt.

00:13:56.960 --> 00:13:57.480
RAIA HADSELL: Yeah, it is.

00:13:57.480 --> 00:13:59.270
That's a bit how
we thought of it,

00:13:59.270 --> 00:14:01.700
that it would be a scavenger
hunt or a sort of a taxi

00:14:01.700 --> 00:14:03.480
task or a courier.

00:14:03.480 --> 00:14:05.855
But you have to go from one
location to another location.

00:14:05.855 --> 00:14:09.200
If you get to that location, you
get given a new place to go to.

00:14:09.200 --> 00:14:11.750
And we just watch the agents,
after they're trained,

00:14:11.750 --> 00:14:13.280
zooming all over the city.

00:14:13.280 --> 00:14:16.100
The interesting thing was
when we started training

00:14:16.100 --> 00:14:20.210
with Central Park, in the
middle of this big area of play,

00:14:20.210 --> 00:14:22.940
usually in New York
it's pretty simple.

00:14:22.940 --> 00:14:25.519
I'm sure some people know New
York City or have visited.

00:14:25.519 --> 00:14:26.310
It's pretty simple.

00:14:26.310 --> 00:14:27.226
You go down the block.

00:14:27.226 --> 00:14:29.094
There's this very
regular street layout.

00:14:29.094 --> 00:14:31.010
But then all of a sudden
there's Central Park.

00:14:31.010 --> 00:14:33.071
And there's only three,
I think, different roads

00:14:33.071 --> 00:14:34.070
that cross Central Park.

00:14:34.070 --> 00:14:36.440
So the agent actually
needs to learn, ah

00:14:36.440 --> 00:14:38.420
if I need to get
across Central Park,

00:14:38.420 --> 00:14:41.180
I'm going to need to take one
of these streets that go across.

00:14:41.180 --> 00:14:43.610
Similarly in London,
the agent needs

00:14:43.610 --> 00:14:46.700
to learn where the bridges over
the Thames, over the river, so

00:14:46.700 --> 00:14:49.700
that it can get from one side
to the other side efficiently.

00:14:49.700 --> 00:14:51.500
And it does learn
all of these things.

00:14:51.500 --> 00:14:52.440
MARK MANDEL: So I'm
guessing that what

00:14:52.440 --> 00:14:54.940
you were talking about before,
with the agents weren't given

00:14:54.940 --> 00:14:57.290
the map beforehand, they
had to learn that while they

00:14:57.290 --> 00:14:58.310
were driving around.

00:14:58.310 --> 00:14:59.518
RAIA HADSELL: Right, exactly.

00:14:59.518 --> 00:15:01.940
So the agent never
sees the map, never

00:15:01.940 --> 00:15:03.800
gets the advantage
of seeing the map.

00:15:03.800 --> 00:15:06.140
It just sees an image of
the street ahead of it.

00:15:06.140 --> 00:15:08.600
And it needs to explore
and sort of load up

00:15:08.600 --> 00:15:10.700
its brain, its
own neural memory,

00:15:10.700 --> 00:15:11.909
with all of this information.

00:15:11.909 --> 00:15:13.283
MELANIE WARRICK:
My understanding

00:15:13.283 --> 00:15:14.900
from the way your
network structure

00:15:14.900 --> 00:15:17.019
works is that it's
modular, and it's

00:15:17.019 --> 00:15:19.560
usable for transfer learning to
be able to move from one city

00:15:19.560 --> 00:15:20.322
to another city.

00:15:20.322 --> 00:15:22.530
RAIA HADSELL: After we
figured out that this did work

00:15:22.530 --> 00:15:24.904
and that we could train an
agent to move around one city,

00:15:24.904 --> 00:15:26.560
we thought, well,
that's interesting,

00:15:26.560 --> 00:15:28.770
but it's doing that a
lot because it's just

00:15:28.770 --> 00:15:29.850
memorizing the city.

00:15:29.850 --> 00:15:32.130
And we said we want to be
able to have an agent that

00:15:32.130 --> 00:15:34.140
can navigate not
just in New York,

00:15:34.140 --> 00:15:35.730
but then in London and in Paris.

00:15:35.730 --> 00:15:38.370
And if it gets to a new
city, there are some things

00:15:38.370 --> 00:15:39.360
that we have to learn.

00:15:39.360 --> 00:15:40.680
We have to learn where
the neighborhoods are

00:15:40.680 --> 00:15:42.450
and what the landmarks look
like, things like this.

00:15:42.450 --> 00:15:44.533
But there's a lot of things
that we carry with us.

00:15:44.533 --> 00:15:46.560
There's a lot of
transfer of information

00:15:46.560 --> 00:15:48.090
that we do from city to city.

00:15:48.090 --> 00:15:49.460
And so we wanted to see
if that was the case.

00:15:49.460 --> 00:15:52.126
So we built an architecture that
was more modular, where there's

00:15:52.126 --> 00:15:54.000
part of it that is
general and that

00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:56.430
is applied to each new
city, and there's part of it

00:15:56.430 --> 00:15:57.810
that's just city-specific.

00:15:57.810 --> 00:16:00.120
And over time, that part
of the neural network

00:16:00.120 --> 00:16:04.260
that is city-specific learns
to encode just the details.

00:16:04.260 --> 00:16:07.860
Where's the Eiffel Tower or
where are the best bridges

00:16:07.860 --> 00:16:08.790
across the Thames?

00:16:08.790 --> 00:16:10.500
What does Central
Park look like?

00:16:10.500 --> 00:16:12.910
Learns these specific things.

00:16:12.910 --> 00:16:15.440
And the two work very well
just being trained together.

00:16:15.440 --> 00:16:17.190
MELANIE WARRICK: And
you can plug and play

00:16:17.190 --> 00:16:18.390
those a little bit more.

00:16:18.390 --> 00:16:20.350
And that's a
recurrent neural net

00:16:20.350 --> 00:16:22.325
that holds the policy
for the location base.

00:16:22.325 --> 00:16:23.450
RAIA HADSELL: That's right.

00:16:23.450 --> 00:16:25.216
It's a LST, a long
short term memory.

00:16:25.216 --> 00:16:27.090
MELANIE WARRICK: I've
heard about grid cells.

00:16:27.090 --> 00:16:29.700
Can you tell us a little bit
about what grid cells are

00:16:29.700 --> 00:16:31.860
and how they play a
role in this research?

00:16:31.860 --> 00:16:35.130
RAIA HADSELL: When we think
about biological navigation

00:16:35.130 --> 00:16:37.770
or biological spatial
understanding,

00:16:37.770 --> 00:16:39.720
we think about
these neurons that

00:16:39.720 --> 00:16:43.470
have been found in the
brains of mice and rats

00:16:43.470 --> 00:16:46.257
that are called place
cells and grid cells.

00:16:46.257 --> 00:16:47.340
There's a few other types.

00:16:47.340 --> 00:16:49.131
There's border cells,
head direction cells,

00:16:49.131 --> 00:16:51.870
but can focus on place
cells and grid cells.

00:16:51.870 --> 00:16:54.570
And there's been
amazing research.

00:16:54.570 --> 00:16:57.570
The Nobel Prize was just won a
few years ago by Edward Moser,

00:16:57.570 --> 00:16:59.310
who discovered the grid cells.

00:16:59.310 --> 00:17:02.010
These are neurons in
two areas of the brain.

00:17:02.010 --> 00:17:05.190
And grid cells are very
general, and they just

00:17:05.190 --> 00:17:07.599
measure how far it is
that we're walking,

00:17:07.599 --> 00:17:09.270
that we're moving along.

00:17:09.270 --> 00:17:12.489
So think about it as if
I walk down a street,

00:17:12.489 --> 00:17:14.280
then I'm going to have
a neuron in my brain

00:17:14.280 --> 00:17:17.130
that's counting every one
meter, and it's firing every one

00:17:17.130 --> 00:17:18.720
meter that I step.

00:17:18.720 --> 00:17:21.089
There's another one that's
firing every meter and a half

00:17:21.089 --> 00:17:24.359
when I take a step, when I
get to each meter and half.

00:17:24.359 --> 00:17:27.390
Another one that might only
fire every 10 meters as I'm

00:17:27.390 --> 00:17:28.800
walking along.

00:17:28.800 --> 00:17:32.040
And these are hexagonally
arranged in the brain,

00:17:32.040 --> 00:17:34.740
so their reactivity
is hexagonal as you

00:17:34.740 --> 00:17:36.420
walk through the environment.

00:17:36.420 --> 00:17:41.770
And they give a way of measuring
both direction and distance.

00:17:41.770 --> 00:17:44.760
So when I take a nice,
complicated path wandering

00:17:44.760 --> 00:17:46.680
around my neighborhood,
say, at home,

00:17:46.680 --> 00:17:49.500
if you ask me at any
point to stop and point

00:17:49.500 --> 00:17:52.320
back to where my home is,
I can just reach out an arm

00:17:52.320 --> 00:17:55.410
and point straight back to
where home is, even if I've

00:17:55.410 --> 00:17:57.780
taken some circuitous route.

00:17:57.780 --> 00:17:59.697
We think that's
because of grid cells.

00:17:59.697 --> 00:18:00.780
Place cells are different.

00:18:00.780 --> 00:18:02.880
Place cells memorize
a specific place.

00:18:02.880 --> 00:18:05.764
You have a place cell that goes
off in the kitchen of your home

00:18:05.764 --> 00:18:07.680
and nowhere else in the
world, or at least you

00:18:07.680 --> 00:18:09.221
can think about it
that way, as being

00:18:09.221 --> 00:18:11.612
a unique identifier for
different places in the world.

00:18:11.612 --> 00:18:12.820
MELANIE WARRICK: Interesting.

00:18:12.820 --> 00:18:13.920
RAIA HADSELL: So
one of the things

00:18:13.920 --> 00:18:15.690
that we found a
few years ago, when

00:18:15.690 --> 00:18:18.150
we started doing this
navigation research using

00:18:18.150 --> 00:18:21.540
recurrent neural nets, is
that if we trained an agent

00:18:21.540 --> 00:18:25.020
to simply move around
an environment,

00:18:25.020 --> 00:18:28.390
accumulate its estimate
of where it was over time,

00:18:28.390 --> 00:18:31.620
so basically be able to do that
pointing task, that what we

00:18:31.620 --> 00:18:33.626
would get is inside
that neural network,

00:18:33.626 --> 00:18:36.000
we would get something that
looked a lot like grid cells.

00:18:36.000 --> 00:18:37.650
And we did some
more tests and did

00:18:37.650 --> 00:18:39.540
a lot of different
experiments, and we just

00:18:39.540 --> 00:18:42.910
published our results
last month in "Nature".

00:18:42.910 --> 00:18:45.990
And this is a joint work
between the neuroscience group

00:18:45.990 --> 00:18:49.060
here at DeepMind and
the deep learning group.

00:18:49.060 --> 00:18:51.120
And it was very
interesting to find

00:18:51.120 --> 00:18:56.130
that these types of neurons
that underpin spatial navigation

00:18:56.130 --> 00:18:59.550
in mice and rats look very,
very similar to the grid

00:18:59.550 --> 00:19:02.160
cells, the neurons that we have
in our completely artificial

00:19:02.160 --> 00:19:03.660
neural networks
that we're training.

00:19:03.660 --> 00:19:05.949
So that points to some
fundamental similarities.

00:19:05.949 --> 00:19:07.740
MARK MANDEL: So I'm
actually quite curious,

00:19:07.740 --> 00:19:09.570
because this is
fascinating, was it

00:19:09.570 --> 00:19:12.560
the plan to build something
that works the way,

00:19:12.560 --> 00:19:13.740
say, like a brain works?

00:19:13.740 --> 00:19:15.540
Or was it that you
were doing this work,

00:19:15.540 --> 00:19:17.040
and we just found
out after the fact

00:19:17.040 --> 00:19:18.790
that oh, this actually
seems to be working

00:19:18.790 --> 00:19:20.400
the same way most brains work?

00:19:20.400 --> 00:19:22.400
RAIA HADSELL: Well, it
was a little bit of both.

00:19:22.400 --> 00:19:24.670
My colleagues Andrea
Banino and Dharshan,

00:19:24.670 --> 00:19:26.910
we were working on this
from the beginning together.

00:19:26.910 --> 00:19:28.451
So they were talking
about grid cells

00:19:28.451 --> 00:19:29.880
and I was talking about robots.

00:19:32.580 --> 00:19:35.400
But we were working on the
same code base, same thing.

00:19:35.400 --> 00:19:39.930
And so we really just started
looking for it at the same time

00:19:39.930 --> 00:19:40.860
that it was there.

00:19:40.860 --> 00:19:42.890
You know, that's
how research works.

00:19:42.890 --> 00:19:44.580
It was an exciting
discovery, though.

00:19:44.580 --> 00:19:46.079
And then it took a
couple more years

00:19:46.079 --> 00:19:49.380
to really convince ourselves
this was what we were seeing,

00:19:49.380 --> 00:19:51.000
and also to demonstrate
if you train

00:19:51.000 --> 00:19:54.240
the neural network in such a way
that these grid cells emerge,

00:19:54.240 --> 00:19:56.730
that the agent that
has those grid cells

00:19:56.730 --> 00:19:59.400
can then do things like
shortcut navigation.

00:19:59.400 --> 00:20:02.040
It can take a shortcut in
the same way that we can.

00:20:02.040 --> 00:20:04.050
Agents that don't
have that are going

00:20:04.050 --> 00:20:05.970
to retrace their
own paths again.

00:20:05.970 --> 00:20:08.460
So it completely
changes the behavior

00:20:08.460 --> 00:20:12.600
of the agent in terms
of being able to point

00:20:12.600 --> 00:20:15.210
towards where you want to go
and find that shortcut if it's

00:20:15.210 --> 00:20:15.990
there.

00:20:15.990 --> 00:20:17.830
A lot of other types
of behavior as well.

00:20:17.830 --> 00:20:19.960
But basically we
were trying to repeat

00:20:19.960 --> 00:20:22.150
some of the fundamental
work experiments that

00:20:22.150 --> 00:20:25.300
were done with rodents and
see if they applied also

00:20:25.300 --> 00:20:25.996
to our agents.

00:20:25.996 --> 00:20:28.120
And just to be clear,
although most of the research

00:20:28.120 --> 00:20:30.910
has been done with
mice and rats,

00:20:30.910 --> 00:20:33.940
neuroscientists are quite
certain that grid cells

00:20:33.940 --> 00:20:36.530
and place cells are something
that exists in all mammals,

00:20:36.530 --> 00:20:37.870
or at least most mammals.

00:20:37.870 --> 00:20:40.780
There are 3D grid cells
in bats, for instance.

00:20:40.780 --> 00:20:43.660
MELANIE WARRICK: So is the
shortcuts one of the surprises

00:20:43.660 --> 00:20:45.070
that you found in the research?

00:20:45.070 --> 00:20:47.170
Is there other
surprises that you've

00:20:47.170 --> 00:20:48.459
seen that you didn't expect?

00:20:48.459 --> 00:20:50.000
RAIA HADSELL: That's
a good question.

00:20:50.000 --> 00:20:52.670
The shortcuts were exciting
because they weren't actually

00:20:52.670 --> 00:20:53.170
surprise.

00:20:53.170 --> 00:20:54.130
We predicted that.

00:20:54.130 --> 00:20:56.110
And then it took us a
long time to come up

00:20:56.110 --> 00:20:59.620
with the experiments that
would actually prove it.

00:20:59.620 --> 00:21:02.210
And that's exactly how
you want it to work.

00:21:02.210 --> 00:21:04.690
You want to have a hypothesis
and then eventually be

00:21:04.690 --> 00:21:07.150
able to demonstrate it.

00:21:07.150 --> 00:21:11.380
I find the reason why I love
doing reinforcement learning

00:21:11.380 --> 00:21:13.810
is because you're
training agents

00:21:13.810 --> 00:21:15.410
that end up with some behavior.

00:21:15.410 --> 00:21:18.430
And you don't know what
that's going to be in advance.

00:21:18.430 --> 00:21:22.030
And you can train
agents that cheat

00:21:22.030 --> 00:21:23.810
in many, many different ways.

00:21:23.810 --> 00:21:27.250
For instance, I work in
manipulation as well,

00:21:27.250 --> 00:21:31.390
with robot manipulation,
trying to pick up objects.

00:21:31.390 --> 00:21:34.420
Think about how do I train
a robot arm to pick up

00:21:34.420 --> 00:21:36.850
a ball off of a tabletop?

00:21:36.850 --> 00:21:38.950
And you say, well, you're
going to give that robot

00:21:38.950 --> 00:21:43.240
a reward if it gets the
ball to 10 centimeters off

00:21:43.240 --> 00:21:44.650
of the tabletop.

00:21:44.650 --> 00:21:47.680
So what does the
agent learn to do?

00:21:47.680 --> 00:21:50.350
It learns to simply flick
the ball, for instance,

00:21:50.350 --> 00:21:53.290
so that it bounces into the
air, which is easier, in a way,

00:21:53.290 --> 00:21:55.090
than actually picking it up.

00:21:55.090 --> 00:21:57.160
Since we use simulators
a lot, agents

00:21:57.160 --> 00:22:00.370
are extremely good at finding
the bugs in the simulator.

00:22:00.370 --> 00:22:02.320
If you want to find a
bug in your simulator,

00:22:02.320 --> 00:22:03.219
you train an agent.

00:22:03.219 --> 00:22:05.510
It's going to find the bug
in the simulator in some way

00:22:05.510 --> 00:22:07.480
of reward hacking, call it.

00:22:07.480 --> 00:22:09.646
MELANIE WARRICK: Sounds
like some good opportunities

00:22:09.646 --> 00:22:10.360
around security.

00:22:10.360 --> 00:22:11.485
RAIA HADSELL: Yes, exactly.

00:22:11.485 --> 00:22:12.945
This is done in
security research.

00:22:12.945 --> 00:22:14.320
MELANIE WARRICK:
So what are some

00:22:14.320 --> 00:22:15.970
of the challenges
you had, especially

00:22:15.970 --> 00:22:17.678
when you were doing
the research in terms

00:22:17.678 --> 00:22:18.970
of street wise navigation?

00:22:18.970 --> 00:22:21.130
RAIA HADSELL: Scaling it
to very large environments

00:22:21.130 --> 00:22:22.450
was definitely challenging.

00:22:22.450 --> 00:22:24.040
We spent a lot of
time just saying,

00:22:24.040 --> 00:22:28.480
how can we run on an area
the size of New York that's

00:22:28.480 --> 00:22:32.590
got 80,000 nodes in
that graph, panoramas

00:22:32.590 --> 00:22:33.940
that we're working with?

00:22:33.940 --> 00:22:35.440
I can give most of
the credit there

00:22:35.440 --> 00:22:38.890
to Piotr Mirowski, who is the
lead author on that paper,

00:22:38.890 --> 00:22:39.970
actually.

00:22:39.970 --> 00:22:43.600
And he really was able to
make that work, both through--

00:22:43.600 --> 00:22:46.390
architecturally, coming
up with better ways

00:22:46.390 --> 00:22:48.940
to manage memory and manage
TensorFlow, et cetera.

00:22:48.940 --> 00:22:52.190
Also, algorithmically training
the agent with a curriculum,

00:22:52.190 --> 00:22:54.970
so it starts out learning
about places that are close by,

00:22:54.970 --> 00:22:58.000
and then its horizons
expand and it eventually

00:22:58.000 --> 00:22:59.980
learns the entire city.

00:22:59.980 --> 00:23:01.020
So that was a challenge.

00:23:01.020 --> 00:23:04.350
Any time that we work with
actual real robots, that's

00:23:04.350 --> 00:23:05.376
a challenge.

00:23:05.376 --> 00:23:07.000
You know, it feels,
at least, like it's

00:23:07.000 --> 00:23:11.350
90% solving problems that
are not interesting, not

00:23:11.350 --> 00:23:14.356
publishable, not
important, but they

00:23:14.356 --> 00:23:15.730
have to be solved
so that you can

00:23:15.730 --> 00:23:18.287
do the 10% of exciting
real research.

00:23:18.287 --> 00:23:19.870
MARK MANDEL: So to
maybe bring it back

00:23:19.870 --> 00:23:21.850
to a practical
level in some ways,

00:23:21.850 --> 00:23:24.250
so this seems to be useful
for navigation stuff.

00:23:24.250 --> 00:23:25.450
Is it just useful there?

00:23:25.450 --> 00:23:27.970
Like where can we see
people, eventually, at least,

00:23:27.970 --> 00:23:29.020
using this research?

00:23:29.020 --> 00:23:32.890
RAIA HADSELL: I think that if I
want to find my way across New

00:23:32.890 --> 00:23:35.590
York, I'm going
to use Google Maps

00:23:35.590 --> 00:23:38.890
and the very standard algorithms
that are used there, very good

00:23:38.890 --> 00:23:40.750
algorithms to do routing.

00:23:40.750 --> 00:23:43.690
But I think that there's reasons
where we might want to say just

00:23:43.690 --> 00:23:46.780
based on the visual
information, what can I see here

00:23:46.780 --> 00:23:48.490
and what can I infer?

00:23:48.490 --> 00:23:51.580
So for instance, a blind
person that wants to navigate

00:23:51.580 --> 00:23:54.430
and wants an assistant
might not just

00:23:54.430 --> 00:23:57.760
want to know where to
turn, which Google Maps can

00:23:57.760 --> 00:24:00.670
give that information, but also
what does the street look like,

00:24:00.670 --> 00:24:04.120
and some other information
about what is there visually,

00:24:04.120 --> 00:24:06.820
or some notion of how
navigation would work

00:24:06.820 --> 00:24:09.080
from the point of a
visually-based algorithm,

00:24:09.080 --> 00:24:12.340
rather than an algorithm that
knows the underlying map.

00:24:12.340 --> 00:24:14.800
There's other interesting ways
in which one could use this.

00:24:14.800 --> 00:24:17.470
If you've ever tried to
navigate around a city

00:24:17.470 --> 00:24:19.215
where you don't
know the language,

00:24:19.215 --> 00:24:21.340
it can make it very hard
because you don't know how

00:24:21.340 --> 00:24:22.631
to read the street signs, even.

00:24:22.631 --> 00:24:25.202
So being told to turn
right on street x

00:24:25.202 --> 00:24:27.160
isn't meaningful if you
don't know the language

00:24:27.160 --> 00:24:28.760
or don't know the
alphabet, in particular.

00:24:28.760 --> 00:24:31.150
So there, I think that we
could give more semantically

00:24:31.150 --> 00:24:34.360
guided information that's
based on the visual appearance

00:24:34.360 --> 00:24:35.360
of the area.

00:24:35.360 --> 00:24:37.210
More fundamentally,
I'm hoping that we

00:24:37.210 --> 00:24:40.570
will be able to continue
to give information

00:24:40.570 --> 00:24:44.620
to the neuroscientists about
the ways in which these things

00:24:44.620 --> 00:24:46.040
might work in the brain.

00:24:46.040 --> 00:24:49.090
One of the applications
there is that they've

00:24:49.090 --> 00:24:51.100
discovered that the
grid cells in the brain

00:24:51.100 --> 00:24:54.620
are very related to
Alzheimer's disease.

00:24:54.620 --> 00:24:57.280
And so there's a strong
relationship there in that,

00:24:57.280 --> 00:25:00.160
if your grid cells are
not performing well,

00:25:00.160 --> 00:25:03.470
then you're more likely to
be in stages of Alzheimer's.

00:25:03.470 --> 00:25:06.290
So I'm not sure what we can
do with that connection.

00:25:06.290 --> 00:25:07.690
But it's very interesting.

00:25:07.690 --> 00:25:07.990
MELANIE WARRICK:
Well, and that's

00:25:07.990 --> 00:25:09.700
how usually research
goes a lot of times.

00:25:09.700 --> 00:25:11.870
You don't know where
that's going to go yet.

00:25:11.870 --> 00:25:15.460
But still, the potential
is exciting in some ways.

00:25:15.460 --> 00:25:18.460
If somebody wanted to
explore the work that you've

00:25:18.460 --> 00:25:20.500
done recently, see
how they could use it

00:25:20.500 --> 00:25:22.390
in their own research,
how would you

00:25:22.390 --> 00:25:24.040
recommend them exploring it?

00:25:24.040 --> 00:25:26.890
What resources would you suggest
that they check out, especially

00:25:26.890 --> 00:25:28.129
for reinforcement learning?

00:25:28.129 --> 00:25:28.920
RAIA HADSELL: Yeah.

00:25:28.920 --> 00:25:32.920
There's a lot of good
material on the DeepMind site.

00:25:32.920 --> 00:25:34.870
We have all of these
blog posts that both

00:25:34.870 --> 00:25:37.780
give you a really nice
high level understanding,

00:25:37.780 --> 00:25:40.750
and also give you some resources
at the end of that blog post

00:25:40.750 --> 00:25:43.660
to drill deeper, to
go to other articles

00:25:43.660 --> 00:25:46.180
or go to the actual
journal publication.

00:25:46.180 --> 00:25:48.044
That's probably
where I would start.

00:25:48.044 --> 00:25:49.460
MELANIE WARRICK:
I'm curious, too,

00:25:49.460 --> 00:25:52.120
what's something that you've
seen in the robotics research

00:25:52.120 --> 00:25:55.840
space in general in the recent
years that would have blown

00:25:55.840 --> 00:25:59.170
your mind several years back
if you think about it now?

00:25:59.170 --> 00:26:01.450
RAIA HADSELL: So the
work that Boston Dynamics

00:26:01.450 --> 00:26:04.660
has done in robotics
has always been amazing

00:26:04.660 --> 00:26:06.190
and years ahead of its time.

00:26:06.190 --> 00:26:10.240
So this is the Atlas robot
that now can do backflips.

00:26:10.240 --> 00:26:14.350
And Spot Mini, the dog-like
robot from Boston Dynamics

00:26:14.350 --> 00:26:18.640
that can open doors and
navigate incredibly well.

00:26:18.640 --> 00:26:20.620
Of course, I am going
to say that these

00:26:20.620 --> 00:26:22.660
don't use any machine
learning, or very

00:26:22.660 --> 00:26:24.050
little machine learning.

00:26:24.050 --> 00:26:25.780
And that's actually
great for us who

00:26:25.780 --> 00:26:27.850
do learning-based
robotics because it

00:26:27.850 --> 00:26:29.620
sets the bar really high.

00:26:29.620 --> 00:26:31.940
And I'm really excited
to see if some point,

00:26:31.940 --> 00:26:35.950
we can have humanoid robots
doing backflips from a machine

00:26:35.950 --> 00:26:38.530
point of view instead of
a optimization and control

00:26:38.530 --> 00:26:40.030
perspective.

00:26:40.030 --> 00:26:42.700
On the learning side,
I think that the work

00:26:42.700 --> 00:26:47.750
of the group at UC Berkeley
has been really amazing.

00:26:47.750 --> 00:26:50.410
They've been able to
have robots do things

00:26:50.410 --> 00:26:53.620
like tie knots in ropes
after a single demonstration

00:26:53.620 --> 00:26:56.950
by a human of the type of knot
that they'd like to be tied,

00:26:56.950 --> 00:26:59.950
or robots opening
door knobs and such.

00:26:59.950 --> 00:27:02.692
Also work that they've done
with the group at Google Brain.

00:27:02.692 --> 00:27:04.150
MELANIE WARRICK:
This is wonderful.

00:27:04.150 --> 00:27:05.957
And I know we're
running low on time.

00:27:05.957 --> 00:27:07.540
Raia, was there
anything else that you

00:27:07.540 --> 00:27:09.581
wanted to mention that we
hadn't already covered?

00:27:09.581 --> 00:27:11.569
Or are you going to be
speaking somewhere soon?

00:27:11.569 --> 00:27:13.610
RAIA HADSELL: I just came
back from a conference,

00:27:13.610 --> 00:27:18.270
so I'm on my way to a summer
school where I'll be talking,

00:27:18.270 --> 00:27:20.620
the Transylvanian Machine
Learning Summer School.

00:27:20.620 --> 00:27:22.960
I don't have any big talks
planned until next year,

00:27:22.960 --> 00:27:23.770
probably.

00:27:23.770 --> 00:27:27.940
But we're just at the beginning
of really understanding AI

00:27:27.940 --> 00:27:29.110
and how--

00:27:29.110 --> 00:27:32.200
there's a lot of things
that we call AI algorithms,

00:27:32.200 --> 00:27:36.047
but real AI is where we start to
understand more about cognition

00:27:36.047 --> 00:27:36.880
and the human brain.

00:27:36.880 --> 00:27:39.505
And I think we're just starting
to scratch the surface of that.

00:27:39.505 --> 00:27:41.170
And I find it
thrilling, not just

00:27:41.170 --> 00:27:45.640
for the types of discoveries
and types of scientific research

00:27:45.640 --> 00:27:48.640
that we can do with this,
but also because it helps

00:27:48.640 --> 00:27:49.849
us understand the human mind.

00:27:49.849 --> 00:27:50.806
MELANIE WARRICK: Great.

00:27:50.806 --> 00:27:52.160
Thank you again for joining us.

00:27:52.160 --> 00:27:54.880
We really appreciate giving
the insights about the research

00:27:54.880 --> 00:27:57.490
you're doing around navigation,
around reinforcement learning.

00:27:57.490 --> 00:27:58.180
RAIA HADSELL: Thanks, Melanie.

00:27:58.180 --> 00:27:58.749
Thanks, Mark.

00:27:58.749 --> 00:28:00.040
MARK MANDEL: Thank you so much.

00:28:00.040 --> 00:28:00.650
MELANIE WARRICK:
Thanks again, Raia,

00:28:00.650 --> 00:28:03.100
for coming onto the podcast,
spending some time with us

00:28:03.100 --> 00:28:05.080
to explain what your
team has been researching

00:28:05.080 --> 00:28:07.120
and getting a little bit more
familiar with reinforcement

00:28:07.120 --> 00:28:07.720
learning.

00:28:07.720 --> 00:28:10.910
I think now it's time for us to
dive into question of the week.

00:28:10.910 --> 00:28:13.660
How do you connect to Google
Cloud Source repository

00:28:13.660 --> 00:28:15.560
to an existing Git repository?

00:28:15.560 --> 00:28:16.450
MARK MANDEL: This is
an interesting question

00:28:16.450 --> 00:28:17.980
and something I've
tackled myself.

00:28:17.980 --> 00:28:20.210
So if you're familiar
with Google Cloud Source

00:28:20.210 --> 00:28:23.290
Repositories, they're basically
our hosted private Git source

00:28:23.290 --> 00:28:24.400
repositories.

00:28:24.400 --> 00:28:27.075
But you may already have, say,
a GitHub repo or a Bitbucket

00:28:27.075 --> 00:28:28.950
repo, or maybe you host
your own git instance

00:28:28.950 --> 00:28:30.908
on something else, maybe
something like GitLab.

00:28:30.908 --> 00:28:33.310
And you want to be able to
connect that existing Git

00:28:33.310 --> 00:28:35.280
repo to a Google Cloud
Source Repository,

00:28:35.280 --> 00:28:36.430
maybe because that's
just where it is.

00:28:36.430 --> 00:28:38.290
You could always use Google
Cloud Source Repository

00:28:38.290 --> 00:28:39.280
by itself, which is fine.

00:28:39.280 --> 00:28:41.590
But maybe you want pull
requests or some other features

00:28:41.590 --> 00:28:42.610
we don't necessarily have.

00:28:42.610 --> 00:28:44.740
But you may want to connect to
Google Cloud Source Repository

00:28:44.740 --> 00:28:46.330
because that's where all
the hooks are for things

00:28:46.330 --> 00:28:48.940
like Container Builder, a
lot of our debugging tools

00:28:48.940 --> 00:28:50.910
that require source,
things like that.

00:28:50.910 --> 00:28:52.550
So there are definitely
a few options.

00:28:52.550 --> 00:28:55.390
So if you're working
on GitHub or Bitbucket,

00:28:55.390 --> 00:28:57.020
we already have integrations.

00:28:57.020 --> 00:28:59.110
So you can basically
go to Connecting

00:28:59.110 --> 00:29:01.900
to a GitHub Repository in
the Google Cloud console.

00:29:01.900 --> 00:29:03.280
We have basically
some drop downs

00:29:03.280 --> 00:29:04.810
that you can fill
in all the details

00:29:04.810 --> 00:29:07.360
for your GitHub or your
Bitbucket repository

00:29:07.360 --> 00:29:08.650
and all that kind of stuff.

00:29:08.650 --> 00:29:10.300
And it'll automatically
synchronize between the two.

00:29:10.300 --> 00:29:11.620
So that's already
pretty straightforward,

00:29:11.620 --> 00:29:12.700
which is pretty nice.

00:29:12.700 --> 00:29:16.474
And as those changes come in
from your outside repository,

00:29:16.474 --> 00:29:18.640
then that can fire off
things like Container Builder

00:29:18.640 --> 00:29:19.514
and things like that.

00:29:19.514 --> 00:29:22.450
But it's also worth noting, and
I put a description in the show

00:29:22.450 --> 00:29:24.421
notes as well, that
you can also manually

00:29:24.421 --> 00:29:26.170
do this as well if you
have a custom setup

00:29:26.170 --> 00:29:27.370
or something like that.

00:29:27.370 --> 00:29:29.960
The way I've done this in the
past, and it works quite well,

00:29:29.960 --> 00:29:32.380
there's a relatively easy way
to marry Git repositories.

00:29:32.380 --> 00:29:34.130
And so you can set up
just a cron job that

00:29:34.130 --> 00:29:35.870
says something
like a GCE instance

00:29:35.870 --> 00:29:38.080
that basically mirrors
the repository,

00:29:38.080 --> 00:29:40.510
and then the cron job will
just immediately fetch

00:29:40.510 --> 00:29:42.850
what's on the
remirror over and over

00:29:42.850 --> 00:29:44.509
again and push those details up.

00:29:44.509 --> 00:29:46.300
And that works really,
really well as well.

00:29:46.300 --> 00:29:47.980
I've done that, and it
works really nicely.

00:29:47.980 --> 00:29:49.420
And then you can hook
up all your things

00:29:49.420 --> 00:29:51.520
that work with Google
Cloud Source Repositories.

00:29:51.520 --> 00:29:54.186
MELANIE WARRICK: Well, thank you
for giving us all that insight.

